        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(cron): bound cron quarantine sidecar file reads",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\n`loadCronQuarantineFile` in `src/cron/store.ts` reads the cron quarantine sidecar with an unbounded `fs.promises.readFile` call. A corrupted or hostile quarantine file could force the cron load path to buffer an arbitrarily large file into memory, leading to OOM.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nQuarantine sidecars are small JSON files. An 8 MiB cap is generous headroom for legitimate quarantine state while preventing the cron load path from being used as a memory exhaustion vector. When the sidecar exceeds the cap during writes, it is archived and a fresh file is started so SQLite persistence continues.\n\n## Changes\n\n1. **Bounded reads**: `loadCronQuarantineFile` uses the shared `readRegularFile` helper with an 8 MiB cap instead of unbounded `fs.promises.readFile`.\n2. **Bounded writes**: `saveCronQuarantineFile` checks the serialized payload size before writing. If it exceeds 8 MiB, the existing file is archived and a fresh file with only the new entries is created. If a single entry alone exceeds the cap, writes fail with a clear error.\n3. **Archive retention**: Archived sidecar files use a fixed name (`*.archive.json`), so repeated overflows overwrite the previous archive instead of creating an unbounded collection of uniquely named files that can exhaust disk.\n4. **Tests**: Regression coverage for bounded reads, oversized write rejection, archive creation and fresh-start recovery, and single-archive retention after repeated overflows.\n\n## User Impact\n\nA corrupted or oversized quarantine sidecar is rejected before OOM. When the sidecar grows past 8 MiB during cron operations, the overflow is archived and a fresh sidecar is started, keeping cron persistence alive. Archives do not accumulate.\n\n## Evidence\n\n### Real behavior proof: bounded quarantine read, archive rotation, and retention\n\n```\n$ ./node_modules/.bin/tsx proof-quarantine-bound.ts\n  ✓ oversized quarantine file is rejected by bounded reader\n  [bounded-read] oversized file rejected (would log warning, skip loading)\n  ✓ normal-sized quarantine file is read successfully\n  ✓ buffer is within the 8 MiB limit\n  ✓ near-cap file exists\n  [simulate] existing quarantine: 8389099 bytes (cap: 8388608)\n  [simulate] payload exceeds cap → archived to cron-quarantine.json.archive.json\n  ✓ archive file was created\n  ✓ fresh quarantine file exists\n  ✓ fresh file has only the new entry\n  ✓ fresh file contains the new entry\n  ✓ archive contains the original entries\n  [retention] first overflow: archive created\n  [retention] second overflow: archive overwritten\n  ✓ only one archive file after repeated overflows\n  [retention] archive files in dir: 1\n\nResults: 10/10 passed, 0 failed\n```\n\nThe simulation demonstrates:\n- An oversized quarantine file is rejected by the bounded reader (would log a subsystem warning and skip loading in production).\n- When the serialized payload exceeds the 8 MiB cap, the existing file is archived and a fresh file with only the new entries is written — keeping cron persistence alive.\n- After repeated overflows, only one archive file is retained. The fixed archive path (`*.archive.json`) prevents an unbounded collection of archives from accumulating on disk.",
